Use `JsonObject = dict[str, object]` and opaque string IDs. Define frozen dataclasses in `contracts.py`: `Project`, `TaskDefinition`, `TaskBrief`, `Attempt`, `Candidate`, `Verification`, `Effect`, `Closure`, `Snapshot`, `MaterializationReceipt`, `Refusal` and `Result`. Their required fields come directly from the full spec's record table; serialize with schema version1. `Result` has `operation_id`, `outcome`, `record_id`, `reason`, `evidence`; outcomes are `ok`, `refused`, `unknown`. Nonlaunch JSON commands emit one result envelope. Launch/continue/repair/refresh commands emit two JSON-lines records: `ack` binding the acknowledged launch and `turn-result` after foreground supervision reconciles terminal turn/tool state; exit code follows the last result. Exit0 means this bounded operation completed,2 means a refused precondition (including an existing UNKNOWN_EFFECT),3 means this operation's own effect outcome/corruption is uncertain. Argument/schema usage errors return exit64 with a USAGE_ERROR result envelope through a custom argparse error handler; exit1 is an unexpected internal failure with retained diagnostic/INTERNAL_ERROR envelope where possible, never success. An absent/truncated envelope is transport failure/unknown, not inferred refusal. No exit code implies task acceptance.

Define `digest(value: JsonObject) -> str` over UTF-8 `json.dumps(..., sort_keys=True, separators=(',', ':'), ensure_ascii=False, allow_nan=False)`; reject duplicate JSON keys, floats and unknown schema versions in digested records. Durations/timestamps are integer milliseconds; resource proof floats stay in separately hashed raw artifacts. Journal records include sequence, previous event digest, operation identity and immutable record digest. All external command APIs accept argv arrays, explicit cwd and timeout; never use `shell=True`.

## Command and evidence contracts

Every command takes explicit `--project-state PATH`. `init` instead takes `--canonical PATH --state PATH --admission FILE`. `status` supports `--format json|text`. Mutations require `--operation-id ID`; each external effect is keyed by `(operation_id, step_name)`. Reusing an ID with a different canonical input digest is refused; a matching retry reconciles the retained effect and never blindly replays it.

| Command | Required additional arguments |
| --- | --- |
| `task import` | `--definitions FILE --task-map-output FILE`; definitions are reviewed mainline inputs, map is output in an owned metadata worktree before reviewed map commit |
| `task start` | `--task ID --brief FILE`; controller assigns seat/attempt IDs |
| `task continue` | `--attempt ID --generation ID --thread ID --assignment FILE`; unchanged admitted brief, no in-flight turn/no native goal, new launch/ack effects and foreground supervision |
| `task checkpoint` | `--attempt ID --generation ID --thread ID --turn ID --item ID --report FILE`; corroborated exact-thread public report digest, bounded summary/evidence, no acceptance |
| `task submit` | `--attempt ID --generation ID --thread ID --turn ID --item ID --commit C --report FILE` |
| `task verify` | `--candidate ID --checks FILE`; checks must match the admitted brief; independent audit/review remains required |
| `task repair` | `--candidate ID --findings FILE`; preserve C1, enforce live-versus-terminal attempt contract |
| `task refresh`, `task takeover`, `task abort` | `--attempt ID --disposition FILE`; stop/work retention evidence required; refresh adds `--brief FILE` at newly admitted base |
| `task land` | `--candidate ID --review FILE`; context-matching independent review and project integration delegation required |
| `task close` | `--task ID --closure FILE`; task52 owns evidence-backed closure, independent of landing |
| `recover` | `--effect ID`; read/compare actual state before bounded reconciliation |
| `cleanup` | `--attempt ID --retention FILE`; validated stop/retention receipts |
| `observe` | `--once` or `--serve --listen 127.0.0.1 --port N`; read-only snapshot/view |

Task50 implements refresh as an explicit retain/stop/retire → newly admitted base/claim/thread/worktree → acknowledged bounded launch operation; no inherited verification. `task takeover` retires the old attempt only; the lead then launches its replacement through a separate `task start`. Each effect and safe release has its own receipt.

`GateAdmission` pins fixture identities, source/coverage/auditor/runtime/resource pins and can exist before a real Sureal Kata project; it cannot dispatch the real project. `ProjectAdmission`, consumed by `init`, additionally pins the actual binding/project/readback and publication authority. They have distinct `kind`/schemas and commands reject interchange. Project admission JSON pins canonical/common-Git-dir/ref, publication policy/remote ref, delegated authority, clean-base evidence, exact tool paths/digests/versions, Kata daemon/project IDs, Codex schema/socket identity, additive Insula recipe/rootfs identity, resource budgets and source/output locations. Resolve actual values at task49 admission, not from historic paths. Check manifests contain argv arrays, exact source/runtime/input pins, outputs and per-stage budgets; neither arbitrary worker commands nor producer `passed` flags can become accepted checks.

Before each live milestone, independently admit A_N and retain/materialize exact X. Each gate then runs:

```bash
python3 /ABS/materialized-X/scripts/collab_live.py gate --ticket 49 --candidate X --candidate-role implementation --materialization /ABS/materialization-X.json --gate-admission /ABS/gate-admission.json --output /ABS/evidence/49/RUN_ID
python3 /ABS/auditor-A49/tests/collab/audit_live.py --ticket 49 --phase gate --candidate X --candidate-role implementation --materialization /ABS/materialization-X.json --gate-admission /ABS/gate-admission.json --evidence /ABS/evidence/49/RUN_ID --output /ABS/audit/49/RUN_ID.json
```

Substitute ticket50/51/52/53.0/54/53 for later gates; `/ABS` paths are explicit execution-owned paths from the actual admission, not installed defaults. The driver records argv/source/runtime/fixture/command logs and exit codes, resource measurements, reopened output hashes and refusal/fault evidence. The independent audit returns exit0 only for exact-context validated evidence; missing gate-phase capabilities/coverage return nonzero with the gate open. Run the same admitted auditor with `--phase post-landing` after exact X/M/F landing and `--phase closure` after required close/readback. Landing requires all gate cases; ticket closure requires all phases; task close is authorized by accepted gate/post plus required retention and its closure-phase result is audited afterward, so a post-landing fact cannot be demanded before the commit lands. A reviewer independently reads the evidence and implementation before manual bootstrap landing; the runtime under development cannot certify itself.

Offline Git/store probes execute inside an additive live Insula CPU runtime. Real Kata/Codex handoffs use actual admitted daemon transports; preserve their call/readback evidence and record which effects execute on the host. Offline unshared networking cannot reach a host daemon. If an adapter runs inside Insula, explicitly admit the required socket/network mounts and prove them live; do not describe host adapter execution as sandboxed. Each gate must include real Insula execution and the ticket's real external effects. Worker Codex worktree access remains cooperative unless actual launch restrictions prove more.

Use the additive runtime entry/build contract below. Never invoke the existing Waymo entry CLI for the new recipe: it hashes the Waymo Dockerfile/requirements and assumes its source layout. Reuse only pinned `launch_plan` and `verify_rootfs` functions plus the complete `resources/` import closure; do not mutate frozen scientific rootfs/images/helpers. Task49 creates the declared collaboration entry, Dockerfile and lock recipe. The manifest separately bounds CPU tests and actual worker operations; it inherits existing caps/locks and preserves extended scientific scoring budgets. It pins required Git/Python/bwrap/Kata/proxy transport capabilities and permits no undeclared writable mounts. Failure to prove a usable runtime is a blocker, not a mock substitution.

## Concrete contracts for review findings

These contracts resolve the initial review; they are requirements for future implementation, not claims of live capability. [Resolution ledger](../../research/reviews/2026-10-03-sureal-collaboration-review-resolution.md) maps every finding to a correction or bounded capability gate.

### Exact candidate and independent audit

For every stage: red tests → implementation → local tests → commit X → controller-owned retention of X → raw materialization of X → live gate → independent audit/review → exact fast-forward X → independent landing readback. Never amend/rebase/squash X after its gate. Changes require X2 and a complete new gate; retained X evidence remains historical. Evidence stays outside source; a later evidence-manifest commit has its own source validation and immutable references, not a claim it is the previously verified code commit.

`materialize_candidate` uses `git ls-tree -rz` and `git cat-file --batch` raw objects, with argv/stdin plumbing, to emit blobs and declared modes/symlink targets without archive attributes, smudge filters, hooks or EOL conversion. It never initializes a linked worktree or carries a `.git` file into the sandbox. Gitlinks are manifest-only, preserving path/mode/commit in the rebuilt tree; registered repository submodules remain excluded from protocol inputs and unusable in this source view. A check requiring any gitlink needs a separately admitted immutable submodule materialization, otherwise it refuses. Reject path escape, unsafe symlink traversal, unknown modes and extra files. Materialization receipt binds C/tree/parent, every blob hash/mode/link, gitlink exclusions, source path and controller writer ownership.

An independent implementation of the tree reconstruction uses Git object hashing/mktree in its own fixture Git directory, not producer manifests alone. Its rebuilt tree must equal C's tree before and after execution; candidate object/tree and source-byte manifest are reopened from retained objects. Controller owns the host copy exclusively, source is read-only in Insula and worker writers are frozen/reconciled. Pre/post hashes detect accidental mutation; this is cooperative integrity, not protection against a privileged hostile host that changes bytes and restores them during execution. Such a threat is outside the specified trust boundary.

The lead runs from an independently admitted controller source. Before each producer stageN, an independent reviewer/verifier author creates auditor-only commit A_N, modifying only `tests/collab/audit_live.py`/`audit_support/` and independently admitted oracle inputs. That author is not the producer implementer. Independently review A_N, test its oracles on deliberately corrupt/good raw fixtures, then exact-land or snapshot-admit it before X_N gates; if landed, select producer baseB after A_N. Invoke audit from its absolute immutable snapshot, never from X or cwd. Producer X_N cannot change auditor paths or admitted coverage/check-manifest authority. The auditor never imports `scripts/_collab`; its digest and reviewer provenance are bound separately from X. Any auditor revision requires new admission and re-audit, not a producer patch. A49 supplies independently admitted raw materialization/retention bootstrap helpers before the product's51 materializer exists. The reviewer is a separate human or model reviewer who did not implement X. `docs/collaboration/coverage.json` is committed with this plan. Cases declare phase and candidate roles (`implementation`, `metadata`, `pilot-state` or `all`). The committed ticket_roles map requires implementation+metadata for49; implementation for50/51/52/53.0/54; pilot-state+metadata for53. Reject unlisted roles; all means every role in that ticket map.49 intentionally reruns shared all-role source/fixture gates for M;53 pilot gates remain pilot-state only, with F independently checked as metadata. Require every case matching phase/role; an empty matching phase refuses. Landing needs complete gate; close needs accepted gate/post/retention; closure audit checks the subsequent actual close/readback. Ticket closure requires accepted gate and post-landing receipts for every role in ticket_roles, followed by the required closure audit for those roles, including each bootstrap landing; an empty phase refuses for those roles. Gate acceptance requires applicable oracles, actual argv/exit/log, exact source/rootfs/resource proof and independently reopened raw Git/Kata/Codex artifacts; missing coverage refuses. `passed` or case-name presence alone is never an oracle.

MetadataM uses the same gate/audit command with `--ticket 49 --candidate M --candidate-role metadata --materialization /ABS/materialization-M.json`, the M materialized driver and the admitted A49 auditor. Its gate proves sole parent, declared binding/map-only diff, issue/spec pins and actual reference digests before M lands. Final pilot-state53 gate binds `--candidate E --candidate-role pilot-state` to C/D/E receipts; finalF uses `--ticket 53 --candidate F --candidate-role metadata --materialization /ABS/materialization-F.json`, proving sole parentE, declared evidence-only scope and all referenced immutable receipt digests before F lands. Both metadata candidates get their own actual post-landing audit; no source hash is self-referential in a manifest.

For controller Git, pin admitted configuration/remote identity and hook digests; use a private empty hooks directory via `-c core.hooksPath=<owned-empty-dir>`, fixed declared environment and `--no-optional-locks` for reads. Admission/submission records ignored paths explicitly with `--ignored`; declared ignored outputs are not verification inputs. Worker-hook/config changes cause a shared-state incident. The observer reads refs/HEAD/index/status without optional locking; no incidental Git index refresh can dirty canonical state.

### Noncircular bootstrap, issue revisions and closure

Task49 code fixture gate/independent audit and exact manual landing happen before real project bootstrap. The lead then prepares a separate reviewed metadata worktree at the new current base:

1. Manually run installed `kata init --project sureal --workspace <metadata-worktree>`; retain pre-intent, command, returned project/alias identity and file/readback hashes as manual bootstrap evidence. No hooks or forced reassign/replace.
2. `init` creates Store/project records using that binding/readback **without requiring a task map**; identity-only state cannot dispatch.
3. `task import` reads the reviewed task definitions at current mainline and creates/reconciles issues through the admitted Kata project. It emits the stable task map into the owned metadata worktree. Include53.0 and53.A/B; do not close historical research issues.
4. Commit binding/map-only candidate M, independently verify exact M and live readbacks, then manually fast-forward M. This is a separately verified metadata landing, not a rewrite of49's code candidate X.
5. Admission records retain their observed transition base; `task start` freshly checks current clean mainline B, the binding/map present at B, task/plan/spec pins and actual dependencies/owner. A map revision is admitted explicitly; the historical Project transition base never masquerades as the current base.

`TaskDefinition` pins reviewed mainline files/content and dependencies but has no issue UID or dispatch authority. Import consumes definitions before mapM exists; only after M lands can `admit_task` produce a `TaskBrief` with issue UID/closure/resource/base pins. Task import updates the same issue UID for an explicitly admitted spec revision. Tests keep an already-running old brief immutable and require refresh/new attempt for its adoption of changed authority. Execution checkboxes are updated only in external runtime/execution copies, never in the pinned mainline spec/plan.

Task52 supplies `task close`; before it is admitted, manual evidence-backed closes49–51 use the installed CLI and separate retained closure/readback. A close requires independent stage-specific acceptance and exact landed/publication evidence where required. Retaining fixture work is sufficient for tickets that do not require disposal;53.0/53 do require independently proved retention/cleanup. Status exists in49 as a read-only identity/queue/effect view;54 adds narrative/health/UI, not the first status implementation. HDFS and cleanup effect recoverers belong to53.0, which creates those effects.

### Additive runtime and actual effect boundaries

Task49 implements the additive runtime recipe/entry and the actual `build` surface, rather than using Waymo's `insula_entry.main`. First independently reopen the retained candidate/materialization and admit the source review, build tools, offline inputs and owned output/resource scope. Run `python3 /ABS/materialized-X/scripts/collab_live.py build --materialization /ABS/materialization-X.json --packages /ABS/offline-packages --tools /ABS/tool-pins.json --output /ABS/runtime/RUN_ID`. The materialization selects the exact candidate's `experiments/collaboration/runtime/Dockerfile` and `requirements.lock`; the lock pins the base image digest and complete package union/hashes. The tools manifest pins the Docker executable, and the driver executes/retains actual build/export argv, context and image identity. An unavailable or changed input refuses. The complete runtime GateAdmission is assembled after the build has supplied its actual rootfs/build manifest and before the live gate; it is not a substitute for prebuild authority. Independent rootfs audit computes `rootfs_identity` from exported regular content/modes/links and binds Dockerfile/requirements/import-closure hashes to a **collaboration** lock; no guessed rootfs digest or scientific rootfs mutation. This CPU runtime must supply Git and compatible Python; actual build/export/ns capabilities are tested in49. This is the bounded [task49 build-interface reconciliation](../../research/reviews/2026-10-04-collaboration-build-interface-reconciliation.md); the original `build-runtime --recipe --gate-admission --inputs --output` spelling is historical and unavailable in X. Build output remains `NOT_AUDITED`, and the independent live gate still decides acceptance.

The runtime manifest records one fresh exclusive cgroup-v2 scope per stage, named `sureal-sustained-collab-<stage>-<attempt>-<run>.scope` to satisfy the frozen scope validator's required prefix. The host lead (and each worker for its owned probe) launches the stage subprocess with pinned `systemd-run --user --scope --unit <unique-name> --property MemoryMax=<reserved-bytes> --property MemorySwapMax=0 -- python3 /ABS/materialized-X/scripts/collab_live.py stage ...`. The stage controller is the sole member before `run_stage` and after all descendants are waited; record actual `/proc` membership/memory.max/swap/events/lifecycle. Stage reservations plus active scientific reservations remain within the unchanged total RAM budget; never move shared app-server/scientific processes into the test scope. Probe scope creation/uniqueness/member counts in49 and each worker's actual invocation in50. Unsupported delegation/kernel membership blocks admission rather than using an unbounded fallback.

Resource-wrapped stage harnesses return normally: use unittest `exit=False` and caught case execution, write inner CLI exits/assertion results/raw logs, then return from the Python main. Expected refusal/nonzero inner commands are data, not a `sys.exit` from the outer stage; independent oracle decides their correctness. An unexpected exception or inner assertion failure is recorded as failed; outer exit0 means only the measurement harness completed and cannot imply case acceptance. If the harness crashes before a resource receipt, retain failure logs/scope disposition and refuse the gate.

The new entry validates its own recipe/lock and imports only pinned `pipeline.insula_entry.launch_plan` and `pipeline.runtime_identity.verify_rootfs` from a mounted read-only Waymo helper snapshot. `sys.path` includes the actual pinned snapshot directory, not a Python module named `waymo-perception`. `launch_plan` receives that reference as `/experiment` and the complete raw candidate as `/source`; before `run_stage`, the new entry rewrites the returned argv's existing PYTHONPATH value to `/source:/experiment` and existing chdir value to `/source`. The independent audit checks the actual final argv, not the original helper plan. `/outputs` is the single writable native stage mount; `/tmp` remains private scratch. Worker argv begins `python /source/<absolute-in-container-script>.py`, compatible with the existing resource wrapper. `/outputs/live.log` is the exact native log required by `run_stage`.

Source freezer writes only to a fresh owned collaboration reference snapshot, outside canonical and scientific frozen roots, with explicit resource-budget/provenance accounting. Freeze/pin the entire `resources/` Python import closure, including command/scoped_stage/stage_accounting/execute_worker and their imports, through the existing source freezer; capture the actual list and refuse changes. Invoke `run_stage` with frozen resource code and separate resource evidence/native output/source directories. Independent audit validates actual bwrap argv, rootfs/import pins, wrapper, host and worker resource artifacts through a separately pinned validator. No socket/network effects run in this offline entry. Kata/Codex and HDFS transfers execute through admitted **host** adapters with their own effect/readback proofs; every milestone also runs its real code/fixtures inside live Insula. Host adapters are not described as sandboxed.

The lock/fsync probe runs on an owned fixture directory on the real configured state filesystem, mounted as the native output. Record host device identity and compare the mounted device; do not use `/tmp` as evidence of host durability. All live outputs use fresh `.../<ticket>/<run-id>/` paths, preserving failures. A fixture daemon uses its own `KATA_HOME`, database and socket, never a shared-database overwrite. Failure to prove daemon isolation blocks capability admission before project writes. Restore uses installed `kata import --input <project-only-export> --target <fresh-fixture-db> --new-instance`; independently inspect the resulting fixture to prove only the intended project/issues/relationships exist. Export/restore/daemon calls run on the host; only their recorded snapshots are inspected in offline Insula. No `--force` on the user's database.

The lead creates fork/native-goal fixtures described in `runtime-probes.json`, in an isolated non-managed thread, only with the recorded explicit per-probe user authorization and actual runtime goal/tool rules. They have no managed task claim; active fixture/model turns still count against the shared capacity2 test budget and must stop before pilot dispatch. `python3 /ABS/materialized-X/scripts/collab_live.py fixture --probe fork|native-goal-states --authorization /ABS/probe-authority.json --output /ABS/probe/RUN_ID` records exact creation/read/status transitions. The observer stays read-only. Missing authorization or unsupported methods blocks that fixture gate; it never mutates an existing managed/scientific goal to manufacture evidence.

### Report corroboration, transport ownership and stop proof

Each external dispatch step has a distinct effect key: reserve, claim, assignment-before-thread, worktree, create-thread, assignment-with-thread (second revision-conditional metadata effect), launch-turn and acknowledge. Both metadata updates read back the exact owner/revision/claim. The controller reads the report digest in the exact bound public thread/turn/item before accepting checkpoint/submission; the item must be worker-authored within the admitted attempt's own turn/item range, never a lead quotation or inherited history; wrong-thread or inherited items refuse even if caller knows another attempt's IDs. CLI IDs are cooperative claims, not authentication. Missing public corroboration remains worker-reported/uncorroborated and cannot authorize control transitions.

V1 managed workers receive bounded task turns and **no native goal**; readback must confirm absence before launch and retirement. Observer supports existing separately authorized native goal fixtures for54. Controller never creates/pauses/completes a native goal merely to free a seat. In no-goal mode, completed turns do not pursue another turn without an explicit admitted lead continuation; an idle between-turn gap retains the attempt claim. A detected externally attached active goal holds UNKNOWN/BLOCKED until separately authorized disposition. Read operations use admitted `thread/goal/get`, `thread/queue/list`, exact thread/turn/items reads and `thread/list` cwd filtering where exposed; schema presence is not live behavior proof.

Task50 probes whether a turn survives proxy disconnect and whether approval requests need a live client. Pin `approvalPolicy=never` and actual sandbox/workspace grants at thread creation; no unattended approval is presumed. Each launch command stays in the foreground and owns its proxy connection for the in-flight bounded turn. The CLI constructs a `Codex` context, passes it to `start_task`, emits and flushes the matching acknowledgement envelope, then calls `Codex.supervise_turn(attempt)` until terminal turn/tools are reconciled, emits the terminal result record and uses its exit code. This final result is operation status, not task acceptance. Two start commands run concurrently as owned lead subprocesses and are counted by the same Store/Kata project. Subsequent repair/refresh launches use the same foreground ownership pattern. These short-lived foreground supervisors record their/runtime identities; it is not a new daemon. It reconnects only to exact retained identities. Unsupported survival/approval policy or supervisor loss holds UNKNOWN, without a duplicate launch. `recover` for a supervise-turn effect reads the retained exact turn/queue/tool facts, confirms the former supervisor stopped, and reattaches read/supervision on that turn; it never calls turn/start as a reconnect. A terminal turn is reconciled with its actual tools. If the live runtime cannot safely reattach, retain UNKNOWN for explicit resolution. Worktree and common-.git write grants for worker commits are recorded honestly; nested Insula capability is proved by each worker's actual invocation.

`stop_owned` requires two consistent observations at least5sec apart: no in-progress turn; native goal absent/non-active; no queued submission/continuation; all owned tool/process handles terminated; no unexplained writer to its worktree/output/common-ref scope. Retain process incarnation `(boot_id,pid,start_time)` plus owned process group/cwd/fd evidence where inspectable; scan is supporting evidence, not a substitute for actual runtime/process ownership. Any missing API, process/queue coverage or continuing writer refuses release/takeover. Before implementing50 or claiming any managed worker, a fixture capability preflight must demonstrate this complete stop evidence and usable isolated Kata daemon. If either fails,50 is an explicit design blocker requiring user-reviewed runtime/stop-policy revision before dispatch; no weakened scan-only release fallback and no indefinitely spawned unretirable managed workers. Common-.git/nested-Insula grant failure similarly blocks before managed launch; no implicit sandbox widening. Separate `read_goal`, `read_queue`, `read_owned_processes` from authorized exact-turn interruption; never stop the shared daemon. Interrupted turn/connection exit alone cannot prove quiescence.

After lost thread creation acknowledgement, recover through paginated read-only `thread/list(cwd=<unique-worktree>)`, creation/runtime/assignment provenance and observed matching sole identity. Ambiguous/missing identity holds the seat; a human disposition must supply actual stop and retained-work evidence, not an unchecked override. No second thread is launched speculatively.

### Deterministic observation and resources

Progress classification is versioned in the admitted brief: a novel independently corroborated source/artifact/gate transition, or a new worker-authored public finding with linked evidence, advances meaningful progress without waiting for lead acknowledgement; the latter remains reported provenance until independent interpretation. A declared check argv changing exit status or novel declared output digest is independently observed progress. Merely changed HEAD/diff, CPU usage, checkpoint chatter, repeated hashes, observer reads and inherited/lead history do not. They may be activity/responsiveness only. Equivalent failure fingerprint is `(argv_digest, exit_code, normalized_public_error_digest, relevant_source_digest)` with no intervening admitted meaningful progress; normalization uses the brief's pinned volatile-field allowlist, not inferred equivalence.

Owned tool liveness uses an explicit collaboration fixture wrapper `tests/collab/heartbeat_worker.py`, implemented/admitted in54, emitting monotonic sequence/time/process-incarnation to owned output every1sec while healthy. The scientific resource wrapper is not assumed to expose a heartbeat; it measures final resource artifacts. The collaboration wrapper can supply that heartbeat sequence plus exact process incarnation and read-only process status. CPU-time/artifact deltas are separately reported job activity; they do not prove semantic progress. Inject a real owned wrapper with responsive but repetitive work for stuck diagnosis and an owned stalled-heartbeat operation with a fresh observer for hung suspicion. Scope the latter to that operation; absent model-thread-specific signals show UNKNOWN coverage. Observer/server responsiveness alone never certifies the worker. HUNG_CONFIRMED still requires explicit technical diagnosis. Preserve600/1800sec progress windows,5sec/3miss probes and2observations/5sec hysteresis; source2sec timeout is not an execution cap.

Source collection runs concurrently every5sec with2sec per-source timeout and separate last-success timestamps. Serving cached snapshots cannot block on a slow source. Summaries persist only public allowlisted metadata/digests/sanitized findings; raw result bodies and credentials are excluded. Any scoped drilldown is separately sanitized. Basic state graphs use the same text graph, with no additional frontend framework.

Resource integration pins the existing `cohort.sustained_controller_lock.acquire_experiment_lock` implementation and actual configured lock inode/path. Read-only validation confirms integration wiring; contention tests hold an owned fixture lock using that exact code. This gate does not acquire, rewrite or release the actual scientific lock, even when idle; real scientific jobs continue using it under their established authority. No simulated lock proof claims new GPU-job admission. No fixture-only proof claims real GPU concurrency.

Task53.0's host HDFS adapter adapts the transfer/readback pattern from pinned `resources/retention.py` and `advanced/archive.py`, not `publish_bundle` directly: that function binds a scientific backend and `retention_audit` fixes a scientific namespace. Pin the Waystone script, Rust executable and native hdfs client/shim closure inspected there. Validate `layout-profile --project sureal --json` returns `project=sureal`, `project_root` and `paths.runs`; use only `paths.runs` inside that project_root, plus immutable `collaboration/<project>/<export-id>/<digest>/` objects. Before each PUT, LS the exact target; existing identical bytes are reconciled by fresh GET, differing bytes refuse. Use pinned no-overwrite/exclusive-create semantics proved in preflight; if the client cannot provide them, block publication rather than race an overwrite. PUT/ack uncertainty is reconciled by LS plus a **fresh** GET/hash of manifest and archive; mismatched existing content refuses overwrite and original data stays retained. Archive verify/rehydrate runs in additive live Insula; independent union/hash audit precedes release. Credentials remain external through existing token-file refresh. Queue import targets only a fresh fixture DB before any explicit restored-project admission.

### Executable pilot check manifests

The two committed manifests in `docs/collaboration/checks/` pin script paths, argv and expected oracles. Task53.0 implements those scripts before pilot baseB is selected. Live driver prepares separate `/outputs/fixtures/<pilot>` Git repositories and fixture daemon exports from admitted sanitized templates; controllers run from pinned source. They cannot allocate extra real worker threads under the live two-seat project. Native-launch/claim/ack/submit examples bind exact outer-pilot live command receipts; completed verify/land/close/cleanup/refresh examples bind independently audited prepilot live fixture receipts at exact runtime B, available before either candidate is submitted; pure/read-only and negative fixture commands execute immediately. Replayed fixture history is marked fixture coverage and cannot satisfy actual-handoff coverage. Prepilot fixture exercises at exact B run before the two actual pilot workers start and retain independently audited command scenarios needing workers; their owned threads are proved stopped/retained before dispatch, and capacity/resource rules still apply. Candidate checks neither launch extra workers nor depend on their own future verification/landing/closure; the final pilot itself proves the two actual handoffs. Every documentation example carries a `collab-example:<scenario-id>` fenced JSON argv with placeholders for fixture paths; checker permits only manifest-listed operations on the owned fixture and verifies actual outcome/ref/queue/effect readback against the declared scenario.

Fixture preparation runs on the lead host: it executes the manifest's host-fixture modes through the pinned controller and real owned fixture adapters, retains raw receipts, then copies an immutable sanitized fixture bundle into the Insula stage native output as `/outputs/fixtures/<pilot>`. It is a subtree of the single `/outputs` mount, not an additional mount. The offline checker only executes its own pure check argv and compares host/preflight/outer-pilot receipts; it cannot run host paths or mutate daemon state. All fixture worker threads count against a shared capacity2 test budget and are quiescent before pilot dispatch. Run fixture preparation with `python3 /ABS/materialized-base/scripts/collab_live.py fixture --pilot 53.A --gate-admission /ABS/gate-admission.json --output /ABS/fixture/RUN_ID`, substituting53.B as needed. Independent candidate commands are `python /source/scripts/collab_checks.py --checks /source/docs/collaboration/checks/53.A.json --document /source/docs/collaboration/operations/README.md --fixture /outputs/fixtures/53.A --output /outputs/checks-53.A.json`, and the corresponding53.B manifest/incident document/output. Preparation and execution pins are part of each candidate's new verification context; D fixtures cannot certify E. The separate audit requires every manifest oracle and raw readback, not the check JSON alone.

